WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:02.787
Making a car jump is one challenge.

2
00:00:03.062 --> 00:00:07.462
Making a safe aircraft that carries people for many flights is another.

3
00:00:07.537 --> 00:00:13.162
Energy storage adds mass. Structures must survive exceptional loads.

4
00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:17.417
A failed propulsion system requires a safe response.

5
00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:21.116
Cities have noise, people and airspace restrictions.

6
00:00:21.637 --> 00:00:26.312
Every extra capability competes with weight, complexity and cost.

7
00:00:26.412 --> 00:00:30.387
Physics does not care how compelling a viral video looks.

8
00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:36.462
Every kilogram of structure, storage, passenger and safety equipment increases the

9
00:00:36.512 --> 00:00:38.311
required supporting force.

10
00:00:38.727 --> 00:00:44.202
Bigger motors may require bigger energy systems, which increase mass again.

11
00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:49.877
Vehicle design is therefore an iterative optimization problem, not a collection of

12
00:00:49.952 --> 00:00:55.902
independent upgrades. Suppose a conceptual rotor aircraft consumes two hundred

13
00:00:55.977 --> 00:00:57.448
kilowatts while hovering.

14
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:03.322
Ten minutes at that power uses about thirty-three kilowatt-hours before reserves and

15
00:01:03.372 --> 00:01:10.122
many losses. Power describes a rate; energy describes capacity over time.

16
00:01:10.422 --> 00:01:16.422
Neither a fast acceleration demonstration nor a momentary hop gives an operational

17
00:01:16.472 --> 00:01:17.543
range figure.

18
00:01:17.963 --> 00:01:24.238
Real urban flight also requires addressing rotor or thruster noise, ground blast,

19
00:01:24.313 --> 00:01:30.263
emergency routes, weather, maintenance, traffic separation and public safety.

20
00:01:30.488 --> 00:01:35.338
The practical vehicle must pass far more tests than the most dramatic maneuver in its

21
00:01:35.388 --> 00:01:39.302
marketing. A compelling prototype is a starting point.
